{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATOR DECISION (2026-09-03): the proposal to track ALERT FATIGUE as its own work item is REJECTED. Operator's reasoning: 'the alerts are a symptom, not a cause.'\n\nRecording that I agree, and why my earlier framing was wrong.\n\nI had written that alert fatigue was 'the dominant cause' of the outage, on the evidence that the alarm fired 11 times in 24h with no action. That mistakes an observation for a defect. The alarm fired 11 times BECAUSE the root disk kept filling and kept crossing 85%. The repeated firing is downstream of the unbounded growth, not a parallel problem: bound the ceiling and the alarm stops firing 11 times a day, at which point there is no fatigue left to remediate. Making it a separate ticket would have treated the symptom of a symptom, and would have produced work — page routing, digesting, threshold tuning — that becomes unnecessary the moment epic `6202-e1c7-c57f-4897` lands.\n\nThe one residual that is real: a threshold of 85% on a volume that legitimately sits high is a tuning question, and an alarm that is continuously breaching carries no information. But that too is downstream — once the ceiling binds, 85% becomes a meaningful boundary again rather than the normal operating point. So it belongs INSIDE the bounded-ceiling work, not beside it.\n\nWhat stands unchanged from the earlier analysis, because it is a measurement rather than an interpretation: the alarm was NOT silent during the outage. It fired OK->ALARM 11 times on 2026-09-01, every one delivered to SNS with ActionsEnabled true, and held continuous ALARM for 3h33m before the outage under BOTH the old and new configuration. The 01:45 self-clear was caused by two genuine 85.0 datapoints against a `>` 85 operator, not by missing data. That is why AC2 was rewritten: the treat_missing_data fix is a real correctness improvement worth +10 minutes of warning, but it was not what stood between this incident and a five-hour outage."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788418201767556001,"uuid":"3c5dd8b8-f8c8-4f31-a72d-4694d33199c8"}